# zurvan-eval: the candidate identity gets no outbound network at all (spec T3, defence in depth).
# pf matches `user` for TCP and UDP only; confidentiality never rests on this rule.
# The candidate identity owns the candidate VM's host processes (lima's hostagent and its user-mode network),
# so the guest's traffic leaves through them and is dropped here. Lima maps the guest gateway onto HOST
# LOOPBACK, so loopback is NOT exempt: the only loopback the candidate identity may reach is the reserved range
# its per-run VM ssh control forward is pinned to (security review 2026-10-05). On lo0 the forward's replies are
# outbound candidate-owned packets too, hence the second rule. It is stateless (pf's default `flags S/SA` on a
# stateful rule matches only an initial SYN, never the SYN-ACK) and requires ACK (`flags A/A`): every reply
# from the forward carries ACK, but a connection-INITIATING SYN never does -- so a candidate-identity process
# that binds a reserved source port still cannot open a connection to another loopback service (security
# review 2026-10-05: with `flags any` it reached the host's mysqld). The range is below the ephemeral ports.
pass out quick on lo0 proto tcp from any to 127.0.0.1 port ${SSH_PORT_LO}:${SSH_PORT_HI} user ${CANDIDATE_USER}
pass out quick on lo0 proto tcp from 127.0.0.1 port ${SSH_PORT_LO}:${SSH_PORT_HI} to 127.0.0.1 user ${CANDIDATE_USER} flags A/A no state
block drop out quick user ${CANDIDATE_USER}
